# Format cheat sheet {id="nm-format"}

::summary{id="nm-format-summary"}
`.noma` is Markdown-like text plus typed `::directive` blocks with stable
IDs. This page is a quick reference. The normative sources are
`docs/spec.noma` (format, v0.19.0) and `docs/spec-agent-protocol-v1.noma`
(patch ops, transcripts, capabilities). What is frozen for v1.0 is listed in
`docs/compatibility.noma` and `PLAN.md` §25.1.
::

## File shape {id="nm-format-file"}

```noma
---
title: Q3 plan
aliases: [q3]
---

# Q3 plan

Plain paragraphs with **bold**, *em*, `code`, [links](https://example.com),
wikilinks like [[q3-risks]], and math like $e = mc^2$.

## Risks {id="q3-risks" aliases="risks,risk-list"}

::risk{id="vendor-lockin" severity="medium" owner="ops"}
We depend on one vendor for the queue.
::
```

- YAML frontmatter sits between `---` lines at the very top. Invalid YAML is reported, not fatal.
- Headings (`#`, `##`, …) become `section` nodes. Their ID is the slugified title unless you set `{id="..."}`.
- Inline Markdown is stored as a string and parsed by each renderer.
- GitHub pipe tables work, with alignment from the separator row. `npm run noma -- fmt <file> --inplace` re-aligns them.
- Fenced code (three or more backticks or tildes) is never parsed as structure.

## Blocks and nesting {id="nm-format-blocks"}

```noma
::grid{columns=2}
:::card{title="Left"}
Child blocks open with one more colon than their parent.
:::

:::card{title="Right"}
Close each block with the same number of colons.
:::
::
```

Attribute grammar (`docs/spec.noma`, "Attribute grammar"):

| Form | Result |
|---|---|
| `key="value"` or `key='value'` | Always a string, never coerced (`version="1.10"` stays `"1.10"`) |
| `key=0.82`, `key=true`, `key=bareword` | Unquoted values are coerced to number or boolean |
| `flag` | Boolean `true` |
| `id=2024` | `id` is never coerced; always the string `"2024"` |
| `\"` and `\\` inside double quotes | Literal `"` and `\`; writers use `serializeAttr` |

## IDs and aliases {id="nm-format-ids"}

- Block IDs are **public API**: agents and tools address blocks by ID. Renaming one is a breaking edit; use the `rename_id` op or add an alias.
- IDs must be unique in a document (duplicates are validator errors). In this wiki every ID also carries the `nm-` prefix so it is unique across the space.
- Aliases resolve to the same node: frontmatter `aliases:` (attached to the first H1), `{aliases="a,b"}` on a heading, a chapter's filename slug, and in book mode the un-prefixed slug.
- A whole-line marker `{#my-id}` before a paragraph, list, or table gives it a persistent ID (experimental; `docs/spec.noma`, "Persistent block and table identity").
- `npm run noma -- ids <file>` prints the registry.

## Common directives {id="nm-format-directives"}

| Group | Names |
|---|---|
| Layout | `hero`, `grid`, `card`, `callout` (`tone=`), `columns`, `tabs`, `accordion`, `toc`, `deck`, `slide`, `notes` |
| Reasoning | `summary`, `claim`, `evidence` (`for=`), `counterevidence`, `decision` (`status=`, `owner=`), `risk` (`severity=`, `owner=`) |
| Agent collaboration | `agent_task` (`scope=`), `todo`, `review`, `comment`, `change_request`, `provenance`, `confidence`, `citation`, `footnote` |
| Data | `dataset`, `table`, `plot`, `metric`, `math`, `mermaid` |
| Wiki macros (Cloud) | `include`, `excerpt`, `children`, `issue`, `issues`, `page-properties` |
| Escape hatches | `html` (sandboxed widget in Cloud), `canvas` |

An unknown directive name is not an error: it renders as a labelled panel.
Any directive can carry `class="..."` from the closed style-token
vocabulary; unknown tokens are warned as `unknown-style-token`. Add
`noverify` to silence validator rules on one block.

## Validation {id="nm-format-validation"}

```bash
npm run noma -- check wiki/noma/format.noma
npm run noma -- check doc.noma --fix               # apply mechanical fixits
npm run noma -- check doc.noma --ignore-rule broken-reference
```

Errors include duplicate IDs and references (`for=`, `[[id]]`) to unknown
IDs. Warnings include `risk` without `owner=` and `evidence` without `for=`.
The parser never rejects input; the validator is where errors live.

## Patch ops {id="nm-format-patch-ops"}

The 25 ops in `schemas/patch-op.schema.json`. `patchSource` rewrites only
the addressed source span; everything else stays byte-identical.

| Area | Ops |
|---|---|
| Blocks | `add_block`, `replace_block`, `replace_body`, `delete_block`, `move_block`, `rename_id`, `update_heading` |
| Attributes | `update_attribute`, `remove_attribute` |
| Review | `add_comment`, `resolve_comment`, `add_change_request`, `add_footnote`, `add_endnote` |
| Tables | `update_table_cell`, `update_table_header_cell`, `insert_table_row`, `delete_table_row`, `insert_table_column`, `delete_table_column` |
| Datasets | `update_dataset_cell`, `insert_dataset_row`, `delete_dataset_row`, `insert_dataset_column`, `delete_dataset_column` |

Any op may carry `baseHash` (sha256 of the target block's source, at least
8 hex chars). If the block changed since it was read, the op fails with
`sha_mismatch` and nothing is written. Edits to other blocks never
conflict. Ops that would inject structure (newlines in attributes, `::`
lines in body content) are rejected.

## The proof loop {id="nm-format-proof-loop"}

```bash
# 1. discover IDs
npm run noma -- ids doc.noma
# 2. prove the change: simulate, validate, hash, preview (exit 1 on failure)
npm run noma -- proof doc.noma --op '{"op":"update_attribute","id":"nm-dec-x","key":"status","value":"accepted"}' --out proof.html
# 3. after a human approves, apply
npm run noma -- patch doc.noma --op '{"op":"update_attribute","id":"nm-dec-x","key":"status","value":"accepted","baseHash":"<hash>"}' --inplace
```

In Noma Cloud the same loop runs as: agent proposes → proof attached →
a **different** person approves (never the proposer or the agent's owner
for runs) → apply checks the current block hash. Agents never write without
approval (`docs/noma-cloud.noma`, "Scoped agents and the shared change
inbox"). Agent tooling: `packages/mcp-server` (`read_doc`, `list_ids`,
`validate_doc`, `patch_block`), `packages/agent-sdk`, and the
`skills/noma-docs/` Claude Code skill.

::callout{tone="info"}
Writing wiki pages: one `# H1` per file, `nm-`-prefixed IDs on headings and
typed blocks, put syntax examples inside fenced code, and run `check` and
`render --to html` before pushing.
::
